  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1250 PM MDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nThe good new in the long term forecast is we are likely to see quite \na bit of precipitation during this period over the western sections \nof the forecast area, especially along the San Juan mountains. By \nSunday, moisture will increase over the southwest US and push to the \nnortheast. Showers will develop over the San Juans by late Sunday as \na weak disturbance over the SW moves over the region. A more \nsubstantial trough is forecast to develop over the Pacific Northwest \nMonday and then slowly amplify and drop south-southeast through the \nweek. This trough promises to bring much more substantial moisture \nto the San Juans by mid week. This troughiness is expected to \ncontinue over the region into next weekend. \n\nFor the remainder of the region, especially the plains, It will \nlikely remain dry and unseasonably warm. Expect max temps on the \nplains to be in the 70s to around 80F during this period, which is \nabout 15 degrees above normal for this time of the year. The best \nchance of some precip over the lower elevations will not occur until \nnext weekend. \n\nAlthough the ContDvd is likely to see quite a bit of precip, temps \nare unseasonably warm, so snow levels will likely be quite high, The \nbest chance for substantial accumulating snow over most of the mtns \nwill not occur until the trough is actually moving over the region, \nand this will not occur until mid to late week. \\/Hodanish",
